{
  "headline": "Advance voter turnout increases for Toronto's 2026 municipal election",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A total of 222,550 eligible voters participated during six days of advance voting for Toronto's 2026 municipal election, which concluded on October 11, 2026, at 7 p.m. According to CBC Toronto, this represents the largest turnout in over a decade and a nearly 72 per cent increase compared to the 129,000 early votes recorded during the 2023 mayoral byelection. Election day is scheduled for October 26, 2026, at which time voters must go to their assigned voting places as indicated on their Voter Information Card or the Toronto Elections website. For those using mail-in ballots, the City of Toronto stated that completed packages must be returned to Toronto Elections by noon on October 14, 2026. To meet this deadline, voters are advised to use one of 25 secure, indoor yellow drop boxes located in each ward across the city. These boxes are monitored by CCTV and emptied daily by staff. Additionally, registration for third party advertisers, which includes any person, corporation, or trade union intending to spend money to promote, support, or oppose a candidate, closed at 4:30 p.m. on October 9, 2026."
    },
    {
      "text": "A recent survey from Liaison Strategies conducted during the advance voting period suggests incumbent Mayor Olivia Chow leads the mayoral race by five points, polling at 51 per cent among decided and leaning voters. Councillor Brad Bradford is polling at 46 per cent. In response to the survey and the high advance turnout, Chow’s team credited the results to the efforts of volunteers campaigning across the city. Conversely, Laryssa Waler, a spokesperson for Bradford’s campaign, stated that the high early turnout indicates voters want a change in leadership."
    }
  ]
}

