{
  "headline": "Majority on Ghana's Foreign Affairs Committee rejects Minority claims over GH¢49.7m South Africa evacuation expenditure"
,
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Majority on Parliament’s Foreign Affairs and Regional Integration Committee in Ghana has rejected allegations from the Minority that the Ministry of Foreign Affairs failed to account for GH¢49.7 million spent on the evacuation of Ghanaians from South Africa. The caucus described the Minority's claims as “dubious political mischief.” Dr. Alfred Okoe Vanderpuije, the Chairman of the Committee, dismissed the allegations and insisted the Ministry had accounted for the expenditure. Dr. Vanderpuije stated that a document exceeding 200 pages, submitted in response to a request under the Right to Information (RTI) law, provides details of the costs incurred during the evacuation exercise. He maintained that the records presented by the Ministry contradict the Minority’s claims of non-accountability. While the Minority insists the Ministry must provide a full account of the funds, the Majority maintains that the submitted documents adequately address the concerns. Consequently, the Majority is calling on the Attorney-General to initiate a forensic investigation into all evacuation exercises undertaken by the former New Patriotic Party (NPP) government."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ghanaian Finance Minister leads delegation to IMF and World Bank meetings in Bangkok",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Ghanaian Minister of Finance, Dr Cassiel Ato Forson, is leading a delegation to the 2026 Annual Meetings of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank Group in Bangkok, Thailand. Scheduled for October 12–18, 2026, the meetings involve finance ministers, central bank governors—including the Governor of the Bank of Ghana, Dr Johnson Pandit Asiama—and other senior global officials to address pressing global economic challenges. According to Graphic Online, this gathering is the first held outside Washington, D.C., since Dr Forson assumed office in 2025. The meetings occur as Ghana prepares to transition from economic stabilisation toward sustained transformation following the conclusion of its $3 billion bailout programme. Dr Forson is expected to engage in bilateral and multilateral discussions with IMF leadership, senior World Bank officials, and other international financial institutions and development partners to reassure investors regarding Ghana's economic outlook and commitment to reforms."
    },
    {
      "text": "The meetings in Bangkok follow a period where Bretton Woods institutions provided positive assessments of Ghana's progress in restoring economic stability after the 2022 crisis. Dr Forson is expected to unveil the government's New Economy programme, described as a blueprint for the country's next phase of economic development, during the presentation of the 2027 Budget and Economic Statement to Parliament in November. Africanews reports that the broader IMF and World Bank meetings, which include activities starting October 10, 2026, take place amid global economic pressures such as energy shocks, rising debt, and the impact of artificial intelligence. IMF chief Kristalina Georgieva noted that the global economy is facing a negative energy supply shock and a positive demand shock from AI, with impacts being highly uneven globally."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "MI5 chief says Russia attempted to interfere in Brexit referendum with little impact",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Ken McCallum, the head of Britain’s domestic intelligence agency MI5, stated on Tuesday that Moscow attempted to interfere in the 2016 Brexit referendum through social media and television reports, but noted a significant difference between what was attempted and what actually resulted. McCallum said the interference had little impact and that neither tactic was particularly sophisticated or successful. During an annual threat update, the spy chief reported that Russian-owned English-language broadcasters RT and Sputnik provided biased anti-migration and anti-EU coverage around the time of the referendum. He also identified a second line of attempted interference involving an unspecified number of internet trolls using fake social media accounts, operating largely on X, to influence the result."
    },
    {
      "text": "Previous studies have cited these interference attempts. The digital campaign group 89up reported that RT and Sputnik published 261 mostly misleading articles regarding immigration and anti-EU issues, which achieved 134 million potential page impressions. Other studies have reported on a state-linked Russian ``troll army'' tweeting about the referendum, though estimates of the number of active accounts have varied between 54 and 150,000. McCallum endorsed comments made by Prime Minister Andy Burnham, who raised the issue of Russian interference over the past two days, though McCallum cautioned that continuing the debate risked providing the Kremlin with a needless propaganda victory."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Bangladesh Education Minister calls for teacher proficiency in technology and AI",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Bangladesh Education Minister Dr. A N M Ehsanul Hoque Milon stated on Tuesday that teachers must acquire comprehensive knowledge of technology and artificial intelligence (AI) to effectively educate students in a technology-driven world. Speaking at the inauguration of an orientation training course for 49th BCS (General Education) cadre officers at the Rural Development Academy (RDA) in Bogura, Milon noted that students are currently highly proficient in information technology, necessitating that teachers update their own technological skills and knowledge. The minister also emphasized the importance of research for improving the education system and developing the teaching community. During the event, Milon urged teachers to perform their duties with sincerity and dedication, rising above political differences to assist in building a modern Bangladesh."
    },
    {
      "text": "The eight-day orientation programme is being attended by 613 newly recruited BCS General Education cadre officers. The event was presided over by Dr. Sardar Md Keramat Ali, Secretary (routine charge) of the Secondary and Higher Education Division, with a welcome address delivered by Prof. Dr. Wasim Md Mezbahaul Haque, Director General of the National Academy for Educational Management (NAEM). Special guests included BNP Executive Committee member Rezaul Karim Badsha, MP; Jatiyatabadi Mohila Dal Vice-President Nazmun Nahar Baby; Director General of the Directorate of Secondary and Higher Education Prof. Dr. Khan Moinuddin Al Mahmud Sohel; and Acting Director General of RDA Bogura Md Abdul Majid Pramanik. Additionally, Milon informed journalists that the government is planning to arrange supplementary examinations within 15 days of publishing Secondary School Certificate (SSC) examination results for students who fail in up to two subjects, an initiative intended to prevent students from losing an entire academic year."
    }
  ]
}

