{"headline": "Toronto cyclists seek Supreme Court of Canada appeal over provincial bike lane removal plan", "pages": 
[
  {
    "text": "Cyclists challenging a plan by the Ontario government to remove major bike lanes in Toronto are seeking leave to appeal to the Supreme Court of Canada. On October 13, 2026, the cycling advocacy charity Cycle Toronto announced it would pursue the appeal, the same day Ontario stated it would begin removing bike lanes along parts of Bloor Street W. in Etobicoke. The legal challenge concerns a provincial plan to replace 19 kilometres of bike lanes with car lanes across three major roads. While a lower court initially ruled the plan was an unconstitutional risk to cyclist safety, the Ontario Court of Appeal overturned that decision in an August ruling, stating the trial judge had improperly engaged in policy-making and political considerations. Cycle Toronto and its co-applicants are being represented by the environmental law charity Ecojustice and the firm Paliare Roland Rosenberg Rothstein LLP. At a Tuesday morning news conference, Michael Longfield, executive director of Cycle Toronto, stated that bike lanes do not cause traffic congestion and alleged that removing them would put lives at risk. Bronwyn Roe, a staff lawyer with Ecojustice, said the group is asking the Supreme Court to hear the case to provide clarity regarding the scope of rights to life and security of the person protected by Section 7 of the Charter."
  }
]
}

